DECtalk v4.61 README file for Linux

For infromation about installing DECtalk, refer to INSTALL

For general information about DECtalk refer to the documentation
in html format in doc/DECtalk/html
in pdf format in doc/DECtalk/pdf
in ps format in doc/DECtalk/ps

Information about DECtalk specific to Linux.

The user that runs a DECtalk application must have write permission
to /dev/dsp. Access to /dev/mixer will allow DECtalk to control the volume

The code for intel hardware has been built on 2 versions of redhat,
5.0 and 6.2.  The 5.0 version may work on newer versions, but the 
6.2 probably will not work on older versions of redhat.

DECtalk is a product licensed by number of concurrent uses.  So,
It creates a file /var/tmp/DECtalk.pids where the current license 
information is stored while DECtalk is running.  If a DECtalk application 
crashes, the file /var/tmp/DECtalk.pids may have to be removed manually,
and only should be removed if no other DECtalk applications are running.
DECtalk tries to free licenses that are used by programs that are
no longer running.

To run the graphical sample programs, you have to have glib 1.2.0.0.7 or later
and gtk 1.2.0.5.2 or later.  Both of these pacakges are available from
http://www.gtk.org/

Currently pause and resume do not work.  This is because the functions
TextToSpeechPause and TextToSpeechResume do not work.

One of the sample programs in the kit is "say"  Rsynth comes with a 
program called "say" and this install may clobber the say from rsynth,
or the say from rsynth will be the one called when the program say is
executed.




Customizing DECtalk voice, rate, volume, and voice shape
--------------------------------------------------------

This repo includes a helper wrapper, `./dectalk-say`, so you can tweak the
bundled `say` binary without remembering every DECtalk flag. It also has an
optional deeper tuner, `./dectalk-tune`, which uses DECtalk's SPDEFS speaker
parameter API for bigger voice changes.

Build the tuner if it is not already present:

    make dectalk-tune

Edit `dectalk-settings.conf` for your normal defaults:

    DECTALK_VOICE=paul
    DECTALK_RATE=180
    DECTALK_VOLUME=90
    DECTALK_LANG=""          # blank uses DECtalk.conf Default_lang

DECtalk's real speaking-rate limit is 600. Values above 600 can make the
engine go silent, so the wrapper clamps anything higher to 600.

Then speak text with those defaults:

    ./dectalk-say "Hello from DECtalk."

Override settings per run:

    ./dectalk-say --voice betty --rate 210 "A faster Betty voice."
    ./dectalk-say --voice kit --rate 260 --volume 80 "Tiny robot voice test."
    ./dectalk-say --rate 999 "This is clamped to 600 instead of going silent."
    ./dectalk-say --out demo.wav --encoding 1 "Save this as a wave file."

Voice aliases accepted by the wrapper:

    0 paul/pablo
    1 betty/berta/beate
    2 harry/humberto/hans
    3 frank/francisco
    4 dennis/domingo/dieter
    5 kit/kid/juanito/karl
    6 ursula
    7 rita
    8 wendy

Deep voice-shaping examples:

    ./dectalk-say --preset robot "Greetings, human."
    ./dectalk-say --preset giant --param roughness=60 "Big rough voice."
    ./dectalk-say --param pitch=260 --param head=55 "Tiny high voice."
    ./dectalk-say --preset whisper --out whisper.wav "Breathy wave output."

Built-in deep presets:

    robot, giant, chipmunk, whisper, rough, flat, bright, dark

List all raw SPDEFS parameters and aliases:

    ./dectalk-say --list-params

Print DECtalk's current/default/low/high parameter values for a voice:

    ./dectalk-say --voice paul --dump-only

You can also save deep defaults in `dectalk-settings.conf`:

    DECTALK_PRESET="giant"
    DECTALK_PARAMS="pitch=85 head=150 roughness=70"

You can still send raw DECtalk commands before or after the text:

    ./dectalk-say --pre "[:nb :ra220]" "Inline commands work too."

Run `./dectalk-say --help` for all wrapper options, or use
`./dectalk-say --raw ...` to pass arguments directly to the original `say`
binary.

If DECtalk prints `dlopen error:libpulse-simple.so.0`, install your distro's
PulseAudio client library package, usually `libpulse0` on Debian/Ubuntu or
`pulseaudio-libs` on Fedora/RHEL.
